Track 2: Betamax Confessional
- Story & Context: Hiding out in the abandoned projection booth of the boarded-up Athena Cinema, Maeve spliced together fragments of old answering machine tapes and confessed her deepest regrets over a creeping, mid-tempo groove. "Erase the tape before the morning shift arrives," she whispered into a distorted vocal mic while the projector flickered uselessly against a blank brick wall. A sudden power surge from the streetlights outside wiped half her recorded vocals, leaving only the ghost of her confession looping endlessly over a sorrowful bagpipe counter-melody. This solitary act of midnight catharsis became the emotional blueprint for every broken-hearted street poet in the scene.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A glowing reel-to-reel tape deck sitting on a dusty velvet cinema seat, with tangled ribbons of brown magnetic tape catching the pale moonlight through a cracked Art Deco window.

Track 3: The Ghost in the Junction
- Story & Context: Years after the great grid blackout of '89, urban mythographers gathered at Junction 4 to pay homage to the phantom bagpiper who allegedly lived inside the traffic circuitry. Old Man Higgins, who claimed to have built the original FM transmitter, swore he heard the instrument's reedy drone echoing from beneath the manhole covers long after the power was cut. "Can you hear the copper singing when the rain falls?" Higgins shouted into the void during a midnight pilgrimage, just as the traffic lights flickered once from red to green in a sequence that defied all municipal logic. The myth cemented the genre's legacy, transforming a prank on city infrastructure into an immortal urban folklore of grief and resistance.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary, misty Glasgow street corner at 3 AM where an antique traffic light inexplicably cycles through all three colors in total silence, casting long, melancholic beams across deserted cobblestones.

